Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:03.350 align:start position:0%
 
the mendez family are doing chores  around their home  the water for the meal they're having  this evening arrived directly to their  home thanks to a community water project  funded in part by the united states  agency for international aid and  development  or usaid running water has been a  life-changing  upgrade for the family  life has changed because we now have  water we used to have to go all the way  down to the hill to collect it  but all thanks to god we now have our  own water  across guatemala impoverished  communities have received billions of  dollars in aid from the united states  over the years  money aimed at improving economic  conditions and discouraging migration  from the country  despite these efforts poverty has only  worsened  jonathan an expert on economic  development says the solution isn't as  simple as pouring more money at the  problem  the truth is the united states is the  country that provides the most support  to guatemala and the three countries of  northern central america  the problem is that no international  cooperation is going to substitute the  need to establish functional public  institutions for the development of  democracy  that is a task that is ultimately up to  the guatemalan government  though some critics argue that  international aid has fallen short of  creating true economic stability  it doesn't mean that development  projects have failed  from this central water tank 95 percent  of homes  in five different indigenous communities  now have access to clean water  the hope of development workers is that  projects like these can be replicated  across all of guatemala  in the village of el tisape community  leaders say the key to making  international development projects like  these successful  is to make them sustainable  we've received training on  sustainability by usaid so that people  can be more autonomous because one day  the donors will leave our community  but now we know how to maintain a  healthy water supply  u.s president joe biden has pledged four  billion dollars to the governments of  guatemala honduras and el salvador to  expand on programs like the the sati  community water project  here the positive impact the program is  having on the health and livelihoods of  residents is clear  what's uncertain is if the biden  strategy will succeed  where previous plans have fallen short  and create the economic conditions  necessary  for central america's poorest families  to truly thrive  now we've known for some time that the  biden plan  to curb migration from central america  is a departure from the  strategies and policies of his  predecessor donald trump now on the one  hand  this visit by u.s vice president kamala  harris to guatemala does represent  a sort of re-establishing of that  relationship between the united states  and the governments of central america  under the guise  of multilateralism but what we're  hearing overwhelmingly from development  experts  here in guatemala specifically is that  ultimately it is up to the guatemalan  government  to make sure that foreign aid isn't lost  to corruption to make sure  that that government that democratic  institutionality doesn't lose  its legitimacy and without that no  amount of foreign aid is going to solve  the problems  that are forcing people in in central  america specifically countries like  guatemala  honduras and el salvador to risk their  lives fleeing north toward the united  states  got it okay thank you so much emmanuel  rappolo for that reporting from the  guatemala  border uh let's um well talks  in fact are expected to focus on the  so-called northern triangle countries in  central america those are guatemala  honduras and el salvador  so most people in the region either live  in poverty or they work in the informal  sector leaving them without support or  protections  the coronavirus pandemic has made their  situation even worse  many are now fleeing to escape crime and  corruption  el salvador and honduras have the  highest homicide rates in the world  and in 2014 the obama administration  gave 750 million dollars to address the  root causes of migration in the northern  triangle in 2019 that aid was frozen by  donald trump  and now president joe biden wants to  increase it to four billion dollars  let's now speak to william lawrence  who's a professor of international  relations at the american university  he's joining us from washington thanks  for your time with us  on al jazeera so um kamala harris's  press secretary  has said that she is picking up where  the then  vice president joe biden left off when  he himself worked  on this portfolio under the obama  administration but  how do you think this time is this time  in fact going to be different than the  attempts by  previous u.s administrations and what's  your sense of what we can actually  expect from this trip  well let me say that these problems are  long-term structural problems that take  years to solve uh but it's much better  to do something as your excellent report  indicated than to not do something  uh and so it's great that kamel harris  and  president biden have negotiated this new  package  of assistance including another 300  million dollars for hurricane relief and  drought relief  uh and then a whole suite of new  cooperative packages and there'll be  several  announcements related to that over the  coming days because  you have to simultaneously both deal  with the border issues where there's  been some improvements despite the surge  and the systemic issues that send so  many migrants headed for the border  uh and that's the right approach and  finally getting that reestablished  in terms of kamala harris she's had some  friction  uh with the president of guatemala he  did some interviews over the weekend in  which she was critical of  some of her approaches including her  focus on corruption which is of course  connected to the development issue  um but this is kamala harris uh  developing with foreign policy chops  it's a priority of the byte  administration  to allow her to have some some lead on  foreign policy issues  in preparation for 2028 or 2024  elections  and so far so good she's she's uh you  know doing all the right things saying  all the right things and getting some  diplomatic practice it's very hard  sometimes for  for politicians to get used to the idea  that sometimes in diplomacy you have to  be  publicly conciliatory and privately  tough uh and that's something that  she'll be practicing during this trip  ray but how would you define success of  this trip when it will obviously take  some time  to see the results of this diplomacy by  kamala harris and the investment that  she's putting forward  so for example the president of  guatemala wants uh to start  extradition of narco-traffickers and  other criminals to u.s  courts that's something they can have an  announcement on new law enforcement and  judicial cooperation  which hasn't been announced that's  something they can cooperate on  the president guatemala sees his primary  issues is narco traffickers and their  deletorious effects on guatemala  honduras and el salvador something we  haven't really done that well on  so on that let's say law enforcement and  security side of the coin  is where we've had the least progress uh  whereas  on the pure economic and developmental  side the pandemic and the vaccine  things related to climate change drought  we've had much more progress but on the  law enforcement side  i think is where they could have the  most important gains going forward  look i mean republicans over in the u.s  are saying that the administration needs  to do much more to stem the flow of  migrants and in fact april  saw a pretty high numbers in fact record  numbers of migrants trying to come  cross over into the u.s so how much  political pressure  is the administration under and kamala  harris herself  to stem the flow of migrants  tremendous political pressure and the  republicans are right about the issue  even if they're politicizing it often in  unfair ways  uh but what happened is that the obama  administration  uh implemented a humane policy that said  basically children at the border  are going to be treated humanely and  integrated into  u.s assistance and you and into their  families a lot of them have relatives  united states  and that's what created the surge as the  president of guatemala said  the coyotes were recruiting literally  the day the policy was announced  so the u.s has to do a much better job  at the border particularly  in regards to to how children are  treated  and to that end the us i think has run  now 30  000 advertisements in the northern  triangle countries to try to stem the  flow  uh it's trying to be clearer on which  children will be repeated back towards  those countries into mexico and it's  trying to reduce the flows but right now  we've had this tremendous surge  and it's you know it's really nobody's  fault it's under international law you  have to treat children humanely  as we saw recently between morocco and  spain i mean you really have to separate  out the children but that creates  these new supply let's say migration  issues that have to be dealt with  in an intelligent way and a lot of  that's about as you've said  economic development and proper  messaging all right thank you so much  for speaking to us from washington dc  william lawrence

